                            <a name="comment206"></a>
<h4 class="boldbyline">
Re: It Just Doesn't Matter
</h4>

<p>
I happened to get this (from an email from the publisher of the local weekly and monthly city mag, of all people) the day after all of our managers and editors met with folks who had done a vast readership survey for our paper.  From what I had heard of the big meeting, I was depressed, and Baker's diatribe restored all my good humors. He also made Nieman $300, because that made me decide by God we could afford to send my writing soul to that big tent revival in Boston.<br />
<br />
It's hard to share exactly what it was about the readership survey results that depressed me, but what I find most offensive is this idea of paint-by-the-numbers journalism. I've no problem with us surveying readers to see what they'd like to read more about, but I'm not sure we compel readers according to some formula. Well, let's see, 80 percent say they feel religion is very important in their lives so you need to have religion coverage 80 percent of the time in your Metro section.<br />
<br />
The most hilarious finding was that, because people find education and religion more important than Sports, that newspapers devote way too many resources to Sports (Someon said, "So you mean we should have a Religion section every day and Sports once a week." Yeah, that'd shoot circulation right through the roof). Which does not explain why, whenever and wherever I go for breakfast, I can find two of every section of discarded newspapers except for one -- Sports. And so (I'm wrapping up now) this is shorthand for my larger point -- what people say is important to them and what people want to read in the newspaper are two entirely different things, it seems to me.<br />
<br />
Thoughts on this rambling missive?
</p>
<div class="posted">Posted by:


		<a href="mailto:zackmcm@bellsouth.net">Zack</a>
	

 at November 14,2005 00:12
</div>
<br><br>

                            <a name="comment207"></a>
<h4 class="boldbyline">
Re: It Just Doesn't Matter
</h4>

<p>
If you're really into what readership studies are all about, and actually I think we all should be, because they can be a weapon for publishers, Charles Layton did a great paper explaining how they work -- how useful and dangerous they can be -- for AJR in 1999. I've got it if anyone wants it. Smart editors break down the numbers enough to know which readers are answering which questions. Loyal readers answer questions one way and skimmers answer questions another way and you need to know who is telling you what. You can make a huge mistake by skimping with a cheap survey -- not sampling enough people to know who's in the pool -- and that's how you get the kind of confusing and superficial results you're talking about. You obviously don't want to drop coverage important to your most loyal readers. Survey also says loyal readers also will read long gripping stories on Sundays, although of course people will check the box that says they want shorter stories. They do. I think you're smart to bring this up, because anyone who cares about what we do has to understand statistics and how readership surveys work, if for no other reason than to be able to dispute the results of a bad survey.
</p>
<div class="posted">Posted by:


		<a href="mailto:benham@sptimes.com">Kelley</a>
	

 at November 14,2005 10:26
</div>
<br><br>

                            <a name="comment208"></a>
<h4 class="boldbyline">
Re: It Just Doesn't Matter
</h4>

<p>
Thanks, Kelley. Googled that AJR article. It's massive. Who has time to read all that?<br />
<br />
Kidding. Actually, it reinforces much of what I presumed about those surveys, and you are right: We need to be armed with exactly that kind of info.<br />
<br />

</p>
<div class="posted">Posted by:


		<a href="mailto:zackmcm@bellsouth.net">Zack</a>
	

 at November 14,2005 21:10
</div>
<br><br>

                            <a name="comment210"></a>
<h4 class="boldbyline">
Re: It Just Doesn't Matter
</h4>

<p>
Layton’s piece in AJR is worth the read just for this:<br />
<br />
Nine readers are in a room at the Dallas Morning News. They’re talking about what they like and what they don’t. Some editors and reporters are stationed behind a one-way mirror – sneaky, sneaky – and listening in on what they have to say.<br />
<br />
A guy named Fred thinks kids need to see more stuff that’s “good.” Someone named Jenny calls it “role model stuff.” Fred agrees: “Role model stuff.”<br />
<br />
The moderator of the group asks what exactly they’re talking about. Someone mentions a story he read about Eagle Scouts. Someone else cites a story on a small business that succeeded “against the odds.” And a third person says something about a story about the zoo.<br />
<br />
Which prompts this back-and-forth behind the mirror.<br />
<br />
An AME: “I’m going to do a story about some Eagle Scouts that start a small business.”<br />
<br />
A religion reporter: “At the zoo.”<br />
<br />
I got a huge kick out of that.<br />
<br />
Anyway …<br />
<br />
I’m no good with numbers so I’m no good with surveys. All I know about numbers is they can say pretty much whatever you want them to say. Which is all I need them to do.<br />
<br />
What most surveys show, it seems to me, is that people don’t pay attention and, um, 59 percent of them are cool with pretty much anything and just about everything.<br />
<br />
Look. We know what’s good and we know what’s not. What’s GOOD, though, doesn’t always match up with what a particular market in a particular time demands. Quality and commercial workability mesh quite nicely some of the time … but not all of the time … and maybe not even most of the time.<br />
<br />
General-interest ANYTHING is having a tough go right now, and will continue to have it tough for the foreseeable future, which, who knows, may or may not turn into for-freakin’-ever.<br />
<br />
So here’s my simple thought on a complicated deal. Let’s just keep working hard and doing as much really, really good stuff as we possibly can, and all those folks out there will continue to read it somewhere, somehow, and hopefully they’ll even pay for it in some way, which would be nice because then someone will presumably keep paying us, which will allow us to keep working hard and doing the aforementioned really, really good stuff. The end.
</p>
<div class="posted">Posted by:


		<a href="mailto:mkruse@sptimes.com">Kruse</a>
	

 at November 16,2005 16:16
</div>
